+### Save the frame on the card
+
+The colourist (or anyone driving FilmCraft over the control bridge) can grab **exactly the
+picture last pushed to the DeckLink card** — the 1920 x 1080 8-bit UYVY buffer after the
+gamma LUT, not the Program monitor's sRGB frame.
+
+**Settings ▸ Playback**, under External Video Output gamma:
+
+- **External Video Output snapshots** (`playback.transmitSnapshotFolder`): the folder to write
+  to. Empty (the default) is `~/Pictures/filmcraft-decklink-snapshots`.
+- **Save the frame on the card…** writes three files next to each other:
+
+  | File | Contents |
+  |---|---|
+  | `.uyvy` | the raw 1920 x 1080 UYVY buffer last handed to the card |
+  | `.json` | width, height, `pixelFormat` (`UYVY`), `range` (`limited`), `matrix` (`BT.709`), `gamma` (`srgbToBt1886` or `asIs`), UTC `timestamp`, sequence `frame` and `timecode` if known, `outputFrame`, `accepted` |
+  | `.png` | the same picture decoded back to full-range 8-bit R'G'B' (the transfer is not undone) |
+
+The command needs External Video Output on and at least one frame already sent (pause on the
+frame you want, then save). Generated names look like
+`filmcraft-decklink-20261118T120000Z-f123`; a second save in the same second gets a `-2`
+suffix. Over the control bridge (`filmcraft --control <port>`), one JSON line:
+
+```json
+{"id": 1, "token": "<FILMCRAFT_CONTROL_TOKEN>", "method": "engine.execute", "params": {"command": "transmit.snapshot", "params": {}}}
+```
+
+An optional `path` is a folder or a file stem (`.uyvy` / `.json` / `.png` suffixes are
+stripped). The reply names the three files:
+
+```json
+{"id": 1, "ok": true, "result": {"uyvy": "/home/you/Pictures/filmcraft-decklink-snapshots/filmcraft-decklink-20261118T120000Z-f123.uyvy", "json": "…/filmcraft-decklink-20261118T120000Z-f123.json", "png": "…/filmcraft-decklink-20261118T120000Z-f123.png"}}
+```
+
+```sh
+# explicit stem
+printf '%s\n' '{"id":2,"token":"'"$FILMCRAFT_CONTROL_TOKEN"'","method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"transmit.snapshot","params":{"path":"/tmp/grade-01"}}}' | nc 127.0.0.1 9876
+```
+
 ### Use a 25p timeline
 
 The card runs at exactly 25 frames per second. For smooth motion:
